1. What is Map in Java?
Map is not a part of Collection hierarchy.
It stores data in key–value pairs.

Key Characteristics:
  Keys are unique
  Values can be duplicate
  No index-based access
  Order depends on implementation
  Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

Interview one-liner:
“Map stores data as key–value pairs where keys are unique.”

2. Map Hierarchy (Must-Know):
Map
 ├── HashMap
 │     └── LinkedHashMap
 ├── Hashtable
 └── SortedMap
        └── NavigableMap
             └── TreeMap

3. Common Map Implementations:
| Map Type          | Order     | Thread-Safe | Allows null        | Best Use        |
| ----------------- | --------- | ----------- | ------------------ | --------------- |
| HashMap           | No order  | ❌           | 1 key, many values | Fast lookup     |
| LinkedHashMap     | Insertion | ❌           | Yes                | Order + fast    |
| TreeMap           | Sorted    | ❌           | ❌ key              | Sorted keys     |
| Hashtable         | No order  | ✅           | ❌                  | Legacy          |
| ConcurrentHashMap | No order  | ✅           | ❌                  | Concurrent apps |

5. LinkedHashMap (Insertion Order)
Map<Integer, String> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();
map.put(3, "C");
map.put(1, "Java");
map.put(2, "Spring");

System.out.println(map);

Output
{3=C, 1=Java, 2=Spring}

6. TreeMap (Sorted Map)
Map<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();
map.put(3, "C");
map.put(1, "Java");
map.put(2, "Spring");

System.out.println(map);

Output
{1=Java, 2=Spring, 3=C}

Key Points:
Sorted by key
❌ No null key
Uses Red-Black Tree
O(log n) operations

7. Hashtable (Legacy):
Map<Integer, String> map = new Hashtable<>();
map.put(1, "Java");
// map.put(null, "Test"); ❌

System.out.println(map);

Thread-safe
Poor performance
Rarely used

8. Important Map Methods (🔥 Interview-Critical)
  Add / Update:
  map.put(1, "Java");
  map.putIfAbsent(2, "Spring");
  
  Read:
  map.get(1);
  map.getOrDefault(3, "NA");
  
  Remove:
  map.remove(1);
  map.remove(2, "Spring");
  
  Check:
  map.containsKey(1);
  map.containsValue("Java");
  
  Size & Clear:
  map.size();
  map.isEmpty();
  map.clear();

9. Iterating Over Map (🔥 Very Common)
Using entrySet (Best)
for (Map.Entry<Integer, String> entry : map.entrySet()) {
    System.out.println(entry.getKey() + " " + entry.getValue());
}

Output
1 Java
2 Spring

Using keySet:
for (Integer key : map.keySet()) {
    System.out.println(key + " " + map.get(key));
}

Using values:
for (String value : map.values()) {
    System.out.println(value);
}

Using Java 8 forEach
map.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println(k + " " + v));

10. HashMap Internal Working (🔥 MUST-KNOW for 4+ yrs)

Uses array + linked list + red-black tree

Uses:
hashCode()
equals()
Put Operation Flow
Compute hash
Find bucket
Compare keys using equals()
Replace or insert

👉 Since Java 8:
Bucket size > 8 → TreeNode (Red-Black Tree)

11. Custom Object as Key in HashMap (🔥 VERY IMPORTANT)
Employee Class:
class Employee {
    int id;

    Employee(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return id;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        Employee e = (Employee) obj;
        return this.id == e.id;
    }
}

Test
Map<Employee, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(new Employee(1), "Sai");
map.put(new Employee(1), "Ram");

System.out.println(map.size());

Output
1
👉 Without overriding hashCode() & equals(), duplicates occur ❗

12. TreeMap with Custom Object
  Using Comparable
  class Employee implements Comparable<Employee> {
      int id;
  
      Employee(int id) {
          this.id = id;
      }
  
      public int compareTo(Employee e) {
          return this.id - e.id;
      }
  }

13. Map vs Set vs List (Interview Favorite)
| Feature    | List  | Set     | Map     |
| ---------- | ----- | ------- | ------- |
| Duplicates | Yes   | No      | Keys No |
| Order      | Yes   | Depends | Depends |
| Access     | Index | No      | Key     |

14. ConcurrentHashMap (🔥 Important)
Map<Integer, String> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
map.put(1, "Java");

Key Points:
Thread-safe
No null key/value
No ConcurrentModificationException
Segment locking (Java 7)
CAS-based (Java 8)

15. Map with Streams (Modern Java)
map.entrySet()
   .stream()
   .filter(e -> e.getKey() > 1)
   .forEach(System.out::println);

16. Immutable Map (Java 9+)
Map<Integer, String> map = Map.of(1, "Java", 2, "Spring");
